WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  five years ago  almost to the day i was sitting on the  floor of a bathroom stall  in ronald reagan airport crying  even pre-covet it's not a spot i'd  recommend  it was a busy monday morning and i was  getting ready to fly out for another  long week of management consulting  after eight months on the job i was used  to feeling stressed  but that morning when i sat down at my  gate  i started to panic my mind went blank my  hands were numb  my heart was racing and i was sweating  with  dread at the thought of another week of  wall-to-wall work  when i talked to my hr person later that  day she told me it sounded like i'd had  an anxiety attack  you can think of that like the  precocious younger sibling of a panic  attack  she comforted me and she said don't  worry this happens  all the time and she was right  it does happen all the time when i tell  my burnout story at the gsb  people often say me too i had a  breakdown at the airport  or they tell me about missing a friend's  wedding or grandparent's funeral to work  on a deal  or how they never had time for therapy  or how they had a relationship or  friendship  unravel during a busy time at work  today i'm going to talk to you about  overwork and burnout in u.s professional  culture  and how we can start to change it  u.s professionals work more than we did  30 years ago  and more than our counterparts and other  wealthy nations  that overwork is linked to increased  rates of stress  heart disease anxiety and depression  a lot of people women in particular quit  their high-powered jobs  because they can't afford to sacrifice  their health or family well-being  even for a job they love  stories like mine happen all the time  you might expect me to talk about  policies that can make this better  what mental health care child care  flexible work arrangements can make  these intense jobs  more bearable and that's an important  question  but i want to get to the root of the  problem and ask why we are all  so convinced that high impact rewarding  work  needs to entail a 60-plus hour week  because i don't think it should  a lot of people assume that working long  hours makes you more productive  i'll use this scientific chart to show  how people think about productivity  a climb to the top fueled by hustle and  stamina  until you reach the pinnacle of  productivity where everything you touch  turns to gold yeah maybe it gets bad if  you overstep things but that probably  won't happen to you  right research on  sleep brain functioning and actual  people at work  all tell a different story i'll use this  chart to show what actually happens  as we ramp up our working hours  there's a stretch where if you're  working intently you can get a lot done  and feel great  but our brains can only handle three or  four hours of deep work per day  once you're working so much that you're  not getting enough sleep you're stressed  your personal life's under pressure  there's a steep trade-off  your critical thinking creativity and  decision making go way downhill  at that point it might be tempting to  work more for  visibility or credibility but it's not  helping your productivity  okay maybe my drawings don't convince  you but i hope that a couple of stanford  professors will  the economist john penceville did a  study on working hours  he found that after 48 hours per week  marginal productivity declines  after 60 hours it turns negative  the organizational behavior expert jeff  pfeffer did a meta analysis  on labor productivity he found  extensive evidence proving that after a  point  reducing working hours actually  increases worker health  performance and productivity  a lot of you may be familiar with  malcolm gladwell's claim that it takes  10 000 hours of practice to reach  mastery  it's based on a study of violin students  what gladwell often leaves out from that  study is what the students do  outside of practice the best students  don't just practice for 10 000 hours  they also get an hour more of sleep per  day on average  and protect their downside  i'm going to pause and take a breath  this is a chance for us to rethink our  assumptions about what hard work  and productivity need to look like the  trade-off between  feeling your best and doing your best  work is almost always a false one  our need for sleep rest and play makes  us human  and it fuels our ability to do creative  world-changing work  but whatever the science says it's hard  to resist overwork in a culture where  looking and feeling busy is such a  powerful signal of success  i also assumed that  success in high powered elite jobs  required overwork  since overwork for me means panic  attacks on a grimy airport floor  i quit my job in consulting i also  counted myself  out of banking pe vc entrepreneurship  law  politics and a lot of startups and i'm  not the only one  counting myself out anyone who wants or  needs to be an active parent  anyone with community or caregiving  responsibilities they can't ignore  anyone who knows their health can't  stand up to around the clock schedule  it's not a coincidence that in countries  with more regulations around working  hours  the gender pay gap is closing faster  than it is  in the us  so now we know us professionals work too  much  it's hurting our well-being and our work  plus normalizing overwork shuts out  certain people  from high-powered jobs  i also want to acknowledge that overwork  is a problem in all parts of the economy  not just the privileged sectors i've  been talking about  and if you depend on overtime or shift  work for survival  there's not a lot of choice in accepting  long hours  that's why it's so crucial that those of  us  who do have a choice in where and how we  work  start to change the culture  so how are we going to do this  overworked culture  can feel immutable normalized by  institutions and traditions that we  don't control  but any culture is the sum of individual  choices and actions  people particularly people with the  influence and access of many gsb  students  can start to spread a new normal  where overwork is an occasional burden  not a prerequisite for success  we can start to do this by doing three  things  first make conscious trade-offs  think for a moment now about what your  ideal week looks like  maybe there's family time volunteer work  exercise knitting whatever  now going forward pay attention to when  work cuts into those other priorities  and make sure that you feel okay about  the trade-off  don't wait 5 or 10 or 30 years of  grinding  to realize the opportunity cost of the  time you spend  working second  vote with your feet choose companies and  managers who respect your time  and who understand that you'll be even  more brilliant  when you have enough sleep rest and play  this method is actually already proven  to work more companies are willing to  consider  things like four-day weeks flexible  hours and remote work because  their talent people like you and me are  asking for it be  part of this change and ask for what you  need to feel your best  and do your best work number three  don't pay it forward when you're a  manager  don't burn out the next generation with  the same traditions and conventions that  burned you out  remember that you can generate more  creativity and better decisions on your  teams  by embracing innate constraints on  people's time and energy  i hope that we can admit at least to  ourselves  that we're not super human we can't do  everything for everyone at all hours  without a cost  when i'm beating myself up because i  can't do it all  i remember that cold gross bathroom  floor in reagan airport  and i think i'm not going to let myself  go back there  and wherever that place of shame and  exhaustion  is for you i don't want you going back  there either  overwork and burnout are not inevitable  we can start to rethink our assumptions  about hard work  and make conscious choices about the  best use of people's time and energy  thank you  you

